From 88982f34ed34cced53ae4ce54b1b9a9befd4fa66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:47:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 678/681] cleanup(copy-drafts): remove the per-slot draft limits
 nothing reads

Base: the previous commit (the budget without host draft limits), on
candidate-37's patch head 5b7cfa37f.

Rationale: once the adaptive budget reads no host draft limits, nothing reads
the per-slot draft limits at all. This removes them:
- CopyDrafter's host mirror (slot_draft_limits), its staging ring and event,
  sync_slot_draft_limits, and add_request's reset of the mirror (with the
  resets it replayed over a later copy);
- the limits' copy in AsyncOutput.attach_copy_draft_alloc;
- the device buffer and copy_alloc's write of it (tilelang_copy);
- AdaptiveVerificationManager's slot_draft_limits argument, and the runner's
  None for it.
The asynchronous delivery of the allocation to the scheduler (the alloc ring,
its events and attach_copy_draft_alloc) is unchanged.

Quality: no change to any step. The budget already took the scheduler's
drafts, and no kernel read the device limits. copy_alloc writes one value less
per request, and the host does one event query and copy less per step.

Tests: test_copy_drafts.py and test_adaptive_verification.py lose the
assertions on the removed limits. The stale-wide test keeps its placeholder
and doubling checks; its sync_slot_draft_limits check goes, as the read is
gone. test_slot_draft_limits_cap_stale_wide_scheduled_drafts becomes
test_the_budget_takes_the_schedulers_drafts, and the two-rank test keeps its
equal budgets. In candidate-37's image, test_copy_drafts.py,
test_copy_drafts_scheduler.py, test_adaptive_verification.py and
test_prepare_kernels.py give 79 passed, 1 skipped (the two-GPU test), as with
the previous commit alone.

Upstream status: local (spark-ds41f). Prepared as a separate commit and not
measured: the experiment measures the previous commit alone.
